From: dArkOS RG52 Mini port
Subject: libretro GL: the default vertex array left bound for RetroArch

RetroArch shares the core's GL context and draws its frame with
glVertexAttribPointer and glEnable/DisableVertexAttribArray on whatever vertex
array is bound, after SwapBuffers returns. SwapBuffers restored the
presenter's state, VAO included, so RetroArch overwrote that VAO's attributes
(its own buffer, the arrays left disabled) and the next present drew a
degenerate quad: the game rendered (its screen texture had the picture) and
the screen stayed black. The state is restored with the default vertex array;
every draw of the core binds its own.

diff --git a/src/citra_libretro/emu_window/libretro_window.cpp b/src/citra_libretro/emu_window/libretro_window.cpp
index 5e26f37..1800e54 100644
--- a/src/citra_libretro/emu_window/libretro_window.cpp
+++ b/src/citra_libretro/emu_window/libretro_window.cpp
@@ -80,6 +80,12 @@ void EmuWindow_LibRetro::SwapBuffers() {
         }
         LibRetro::UploadVideoFrame(RETRO_HW_FRAME_BUFFER_VALID, static_cast<unsigned>(width),
                                    static_cast<unsigned>(height), 0);
+        // dArkOS: RetroArch shares this context and draws its frame with glVertexAttribPointer and
+        // glEnable/DisableVertexAttribArray on whatever vertex array is bound, after this returns.
+        // Restoring the presenter's VAO here let it overwrite that VAO's attributes (its own buffer,
+        // arrays left disabled): the next present drew a degenerate quad and the screen stayed
+        // black. Leave the default vertex array bound; every draw of ours binds its own.
+        current_state.draw.vertex_array = 0;
         current_state.Apply();
 #endif
         break;
